Why: Muse (muse.ai) exposes no public consumer API and its chat runs over a private
WebSocket (wss://edge-chat.meta.ai), so the only honest way to use it programmatically
is to drive the real web UI in a real browser. The backend is therefore Firefox/Gecko
(system Firefox 159.0.1) via geckodriver 0.37.1 (W3C WebDriver) with a minimal in-house
client — no Chromium-bundled automation framework.

Key choices grounded in verification:
- geckodriver 0.37.1 rejects moz:firefoxOptions.profile ("Invalid padding"), so the
  profile is passed as a -profile arg (cookie persistence across restarts confirmed for
  cookies with real expiry). Login is interactive/headed once; session lives in profile.
- Replies are extracted by polling the rendered transcript DOM (private protocol not
  observable) and streamed as deltas; prompt-echo and previous-turn leaks are guarded.
- Selectors are ordered/fallback and env-overridable because Muse ships markup changes.
Verified: pnpm gecko:check (E2E against fixture), pnpm check (tsc, 10/10 vitest, next build).
